♫ I wake up every morning

00:08:09.544 --> 00:08:13.242
♫ Entspresso keep me goin'

00:08:13.242 --> 00:08:15.325
(typing)

00:08:16.462 --> 00:08:19.265
- My assistant's got a
great thing now on this.

00:08:19.265 --> 00:08:21.519
Once in every four months,
somebody sends an email

00:08:21.519 --> 00:08:23.242
saying, "I've got a huge
startup that I want you

00:08:23.242 --> 00:08:27.159
to invest in, but you've
got to sign this NDA".

00:08:28.057 --> 00:08:30.159
Which literally, every time,
gets an email back that says

00:08:30.159 --> 00:08:32.006
(censored) you, right?

00:08:32.006 --> 00:08:35.739
And the reason is, I'm a
humongous believer that ideas

00:08:35.739 --> 00:08:38.473
are (censored), and that
execution's the game.

00:08:38.473 --> 00:08:40.158
We've all got ideas.

00:08:40.158 --> 00:08:41.641
Everybody's got ideas.

00:08:41.641 --> 00:08:43.836
Do you know how many (censored)
ideas we all have here?

00:08:43.836 --> 00:08:45.855
We could probably sit here
for the next two hours,

00:08:45.855 --> 00:08:48.874
draw them all out, record
'em, and predict the next 78

00:08:48.874 --> 00:08:51.540
great startups over the next nine years.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:53.151
And?

00:08:53.151 --> 00:08:57.096
So, I think the thing
that is another theme

00:08:57.096 --> 00:09:00.225
in entrepreneurship is
there is way too much

00:09:00.225 --> 00:09:02.979
fodder brought to the idea.

00:09:02.979 --> 00:09:06.192
Uber was Magic Cab, three years earlier.

00:09:06.192 --> 00:09:07.424
Uber is not an idea.

00:09:07.424 --> 00:09:10.021
Uber existed, it's called Magic Cab.

00:09:10.021 --> 00:09:12.354
But the guys that execute it, sucked.

00:09:12.354 --> 00:09:13.806
So they lost.

00:09:13.806 --> 00:09:17.125
So I think, you know, if
there's any level of romance

00:09:17.125 --> 00:09:19.009
left in this room about your idea,

00:09:19.009 --> 00:09:20.736
I'd like to suffocate it.

00:09:20.736 --> 00:09:23.294
Because I think the actual situation is

00:09:23.294 --> 00:09:25.142
what you actually do with it.

00:09:25.142 --> 00:09:27.748
- I mean the reality is,
reading a book or going

00:09:27.748 --> 00:09:30.536
to a conference, or having
a great conversation

00:09:30.536 --> 00:09:34.284
where you get this golden
information, that's all fantastic.

00:09:34.284 --> 00:09:38.646
But what makes mastery is
execution on the ideas,

00:09:38.646 --> 00:09:39.813
not the ideas.

00:09:40.904 --> 00:09:43.080
And so no idea works
unless you're willing to

00:09:43.080 --> 00:09:46.734
roll up your sleeves, do the
practice, invest the time,

00:09:46.734 --> 00:09:49.084
put in the effort, do the work.

00:09:49.084 --> 00:09:52.038
I think we've all observed
a lot of people who,

00:09:52.038 --> 00:09:54.705
they love reading the
books, they love showing up

00:09:54.705 --> 00:09:57.424
at the courses, they do
all the online training,

00:09:57.424 --> 00:09:58.941
and nothing ever changes.

00:09:58.941 --> 00:10:00.588
And they say, well, you know, I don't know

00:10:00.588 --> 00:10:03.221
why it doesn't change, why
my life doesn't change,

00:10:03.221 --> 00:10:06.837
why my thinking doesn't
change, why my performance

00:10:06.837 --> 00:10:10.807
doesn't change, why my
relationships don't change.

00:10:10.807 --> 00:10:13.754
Well, it's because ideas
don't work if you don't

00:10:13.754 --> 00:10:15.192
execute on them.

00:10:15.192 --> 00:10:16.983
So if you look at the
great business builders,

00:10:16.983 --> 00:10:19.831
you look at any great performer,

00:10:19.831 --> 00:10:22.503
one thing that makes them great,

00:10:22.503 --> 00:10:24.059
is their grit.

00:10:24.059 --> 00:10:26.022
One thing that makes them great,

00:10:26.022 --> 00:10:28.614
is their hunger to practice.

00:10:28.614 --> 00:10:30.543
One thing that makes them great,

00:10:30.543 --> 00:10:33.105
is their willing to sacrifice.

00:10:33.105 --> 00:10:35.694
I mean, yes, they're passionate,
but did you know the root

00:10:35.694 --> 00:10:37.820
of the word passion, is suffering.

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:41.602
You've got to be willing
to suffer for your vision.

00:10:41.602 --> 00:10:44.345
You've got to be willing
to suffer to reach

00:10:44.345 --> 00:10:46.201
BIW (Best In World).

00:10:46.201 --> 00:10:49.510
You've got to be willing to
suffer the ridicule and laughter

00:10:49.510 --> 00:10:52.796
of your critics and your
cynics to get to a place

00:10:52.796 --> 00:10:54.399
called world-class.

00:10:54.399 --> 00:10:57.067
- I mean not a lot of people
know that you are a fantastic

00:10:57.067 --> 00:10:59.585
direct response marketer,
with your mail-order business.

00:10:59.585 --> 00:11:01.805
Can you talk to us about
that dollar-into-two concept?

00:11:01.805 --> 00:11:04.722
- Well, you know, we all pay taxes.

00:11:05.661 --> 00:11:10.376
And we all hate the idea
that we're losing that money.

00:11:10.376 --> 00:11:13.126
But there's a difference
between hating that you

00:11:13.126 --> 00:11:16.295
lose that money, and hating to pay taxes.

00:11:16.295 --> 00:11:18.508
I love paying taxes.

00:11:18.508 --> 00:11:21.839
If it is the fair amount,
and if it is used wisely,

00:11:21.839 --> 00:11:24.591
I think we all have to contribute
it, because it's how you

00:11:24.591 --> 00:11:26.928
set up social programs, it's
how you build infrastructure,

00:11:26.928 --> 00:11:29.265
it's how you have a military
and all of this kind of thing.

00:11:29.265 --> 00:11:32.650
So, that's great, but
I always had the belief

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:36.166
that the money that I pay
in taxes, I want to make up

00:11:36.166 --> 00:11:38.221
through wise investments.

00:11:38.221 --> 00:11:40.150
So this is why I got into
real estate right away

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:43.733
in the '70's, and I
started buying raw land

00:11:44.926 --> 00:11:46.627
and developing that.

00:11:46.627 --> 00:11:50.119
I started buying apartment
buildings and you know,

00:11:50.119 --> 00:11:54.391
just think about it, I remember
in 1974 I bought my first

00:11:54.391 --> 00:11:55.924
apartment building,

00:11:55.924 --> 00:11:58.424
and we bought it for $215,000,

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:02.402
that was then a lot of money.

00:12:02.402 --> 00:12:03.235
- Yeah

00:12:03.235 --> 00:12:07.402
- And I put only down
like maybe $35 - $37,000,

00:12:08.454 --> 00:12:11.743
but within two years, the
apartment building was worth,

00:12:11.743 --> 00:12:16.311
instead of the $215,
$350,000, and I sold it.

00:12:16.311 --> 00:12:19.105
So imagine now how much gain there was.

00:12:19.105 --> 00:12:19.938
- (Interviewer) Yeah

00:12:19.938 --> 00:12:23.682
- I mean, so we made hundreds
of percent on my initial

00:12:23.682 --> 00:12:26.363
investment, so that's
what I'm talking about.

00:12:26.363 --> 00:12:30.530
See it takes thoughts and
ideas can make much more money

00:12:34.706 --> 00:12:36.839
than actual physical labor.

00:12:36.839 --> 00:12:37.672
- Um hm

00:12:37.672 --> 00:12:40.583
- And physical labor is always
part of the action because

00:12:40.583 --> 00:12:42.302
I always say you've got to
work your ass off no matter

00:12:42.302 --> 00:12:44.611
what you do, because now you
have a great idea, but then

00:12:44.611 --> 00:12:46.699
you still have to implement
it, and you have to hassle,

00:12:46.699 --> 00:12:48.431
you have to go the bank, get
their financial statement,

00:12:48.431 --> 00:12:51.292
do this and that, and raise
money sometimes, and you go

00:12:51.292 --> 00:12:53.346
to look at a thousand
apartment buildings to find

00:12:53.346 --> 00:12:55.235
the right one and all
of these kind of things.

00:12:55.235 --> 00:12:56.837
So you still have to work.

00:12:56.837 --> 00:12:59.959
But normally if it's just
physical labor, you can never

00:12:59.959 --> 00:13:02.994
make the money that you can
make when you have ideas.

00:13:02.994 --> 00:13:05.175
This is where it's important,
I always tell people, I say,

00:13:05.175 --> 00:13:09.403
"the secret is, there are
certain times, don't think."

00:13:09.403 --> 00:13:11.654
Like when you get up in
the morning, don't think.

00:13:11.654 --> 00:13:13.865
Just roll out of bed, go
on your Lifecycle, or go on

00:13:13.865 --> 00:13:16.562
a bicycle ride, or go to
the gym, workout, you know

00:13:16.562 --> 00:13:17.789
that's what you have to do.

00:13:17.789 --> 00:13:19.846
And then read something
and learn something.

00:13:19.846 --> 00:13:21.564
So don't even thing about it.

00:13:21.564 --> 00:13:22.397
- Yeah

00:13:22.397 --> 00:13:23.893
- But there's other times
when you have to really think

00:13:23.893 --> 00:13:26.320
and you really have to get
creative and have a clear vision

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:28.267
of where you want to go
and what you want to do.

00:13:28.267 --> 00:13:29.828
And that's what I always had,

00:13:29.828 --> 00:13:31.067
and that's what I always believed in.

00:13:31.067 --> 00:13:35.344
I always believed in making
money so that then you can

00:13:35.344 --> 00:13:36.577
do something with that.

00:13:36.577 --> 00:13:38.867
You invest in it in orders,
eventually you create a family,

00:13:38.867 --> 00:13:40.833
you give some of it to your
kids and all that stuff.

00:13:40.833 --> 00:13:42.274
But also for charity.

00:13:42.274 --> 00:13:45.182
Look, I have the Schwarzenegger
Institute at USC.

00:13:45.182 --> 00:13:46.015
- (Interviewer) Yeah

00:13:46.015 --> 00:13:47.685
- So we have to raise money
all the time and big money

00:13:47.685 --> 00:13:50.372
to pay for that and to support that.

00:13:50.372 --> 00:13:52.515
We have the R20, the
environmental organization,

00:13:52.515 --> 00:13:55.550
or any organization of any idea's great,

00:13:55.550 --> 00:13:56.615
but if you don't have the money,

00:13:56.615 --> 00:13:58.306
you can't implement anything.

00:13:58.306 --> 00:14:00.841
And so, this is why it is
important that if it is

00:14:00.841 --> 00:14:03.599
an environmental organization
or if it is my afterschool

00:14:03.599 --> 00:14:06.759
programs, that are nationwide,
where we raise millions

00:14:06.759 --> 00:14:08.434
and millions of dollars every year.

00:14:08.434 --> 00:14:11.967
Or if it just, you know,
living in a great mansion

00:14:11.967 --> 00:14:14.061
or driving a nice Bentley in the Bulgari.

00:14:14.061 --> 00:14:16.435
You know, this, the kind of
things, it takes money to do all

00:14:16.435 --> 00:14:17.268
of this kind of stuff.

00:14:17.268 --> 00:14:19.933
So therefore, I believe in
making one dollar into two,

00:14:19.933 --> 00:14:23.223
and having a good time
when I pay my taxes.

00:14:23.223 --> 00:14:24.056
(laughs)
- So when someone says,

00:14:24.056 --> 00:14:27.555
"yes, yes, Arnold, I
say, we have bad news".

00:14:27.555 --> 00:14:30.880
"You owe 50 million dollars in taxes".

00:14:30.880 --> 00:14:32.380
I say, "wow, yes!"

00:14:34.527 --> 00:14:35.756
And then they look at me and says,

00:14:35.756 --> 00:14:37.211
"why are you happy about that?"

00:14:37.211 --> 00:14:38.044
- (Interviewer) He's finally lost it.

00:14:38.044 --> 00:14:40.806
- Because that means you must
have made a lot of money.

00:14:40.806 --> 00:14:43.017
You have to pay that much money in taxes.

00:14:43.017 --> 00:14:45.271
And they say, "yeah, that's
a way to look at it".

00:14:45.271 --> 00:14:46.325
So that's the way I look at it.

